  "raw": {
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/853c0ac9a0b9-assistant-professor-of-creative-writing-creative-nonfiction": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Creative Nonfiction",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Creative Nonfiction at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Creative Nonfiction Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Creative Nonfiction %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a faculty position in Creative Writing with a specialization in creative nonfiction. This is an Assistant Professor position in an extended contract system with a 24 credit teaching load (typically a 3-3 class load) beginning in August of 2027. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. We seek a broadly-trained creative writer with expertise in creative nonfiction, who is strongly committed to teaching and student-engaged research in an undergraduate liberal arts environment. Position Description We offer a creative writing major and minor with emphasis in poetry, fiction, and creative nonfiction. Our curriculum is research-focused and critically examines the cultural implications of craft. The Creative Writing program is housed in the Creative Arts department with Art and Music and offers interdisciplinary courses suitable for all artists in the department. The undergraduate Creative Writing program enjoys a close relationship with the renowned Warren Wilson MFA Program for Writers, including an undergraduate course where students participate in an MFA residency and a yearly Writer-in-Residence drawn from the MFA faculty. In addition, we support a lively writing community by emphasizing literary citizenship in our courses, holding a reading series, and producing a literary journal. All Creative Writing faculty teach broadly, with courses that include multi-genre classes and single-genre workshops. Faculty in this position would develop introductory and advanced courses in creative nonfiction that seek to expose students to the breadth of the genre; to a diverse group of writers and styles of texts; and to key issues, controversies, and critiques in the field. Responsibilities also include teaching Literary Magazine History and Editing course in which students produce Peal , our literary magazine, and would also be expected to develop reading courses in their area of expertise aimed at diversifying our students’ exposure to contemporary literature. In addition, faculty have opportunities to teach study-away courses, intensive “lightning term” courses, and first-year seminars. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours of undergraduate instruction per academic year. Provide academic and career advising to students. Contribute to the college’s General Education Program, as determined to be appropriate in consultation with the Department and Program Chairs. Provide service to the Department and the College. Engage in ongoing professional development, scholarly, and/or creative activity. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D and/or MFA in Creative Writing (not Literature). ABD will be considered; Experience teaching creative writing at the college level, or equivalent; Demonstrated experience with advancing a learning environment that is conducive to all students. For instance, experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities, course content, and subject matter experts, and working with diverse student populations; Evidence of high quality publication in creative nonfiction through journals, magazines, presses, or other recognized venues; Experience with student-run literary magazines. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of demonstrated teaching excellence at the undergraduate level; At least one full-length creative nonfiction book with a nationally recognized publisher; Ph.D and/or MFA with a focus in creative nonfiction. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: a curriculum vitae A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or creative scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by November 15, 2026 . Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/b3ff49e7478a-assistant-professor-of-creative-writing-poetry": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Poetry",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Poetry at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Poetry Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Creative Writing, Poetry %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a faculty position in Creative Writing with a specialization in poetry. This is an Assistant Professor position in an extended contract system with a 24 credit teaching load (typically a 3-3 class load) beginning in August of 2027. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. We seek a broadly-trained creative writer with expertise in poetry, who is strongly committed to teaching and student-engaged research in an undergraduate liberal arts environment. Position Description We offer a Creative Writing major and minor with emphasis in poetry, fiction, and creative nonfiction. Our curriculum is research-focused and critically examines the cultural implications of craft. The Creative Writing program is housed in the Creative Arts department with Art and Music and offers interdisciplinary courses suitable for all artists in the department. The undergraduate Creative Writing program enjoys a close relationship with the renowned Warren Wilson MFA Program for Writers, including an undergraduate course where students participate in an MFA residency and a yearly Writer-in-Residence drawn from the MFA faculty. In addition, we support a lively writing community by emphasizing literary citizenship in our courses, holding a reading series, and producing a literary journal. All Creative Writing faculty teach broadly, with courses that include multi-genre classes and single-genre workshops. Faculty in this position would develop introductory and advanced courses in poetry that seek to expose students to the breadth of the genre; to a diverse group of writers and styles of texts; and to key issues, controversies, and critiques in the field. Responsibilities also include teaching our Literary Magazine History and Editing course in which students produce Peal , our literary magazine, and would also be expected to develop reading courses in their area of expertise aimed at diversifying our students’ exposure to contemporary literature. In addition, faculty have opportunities to teach study-away courses, intensive “lightning term” courses, and first-year seminars. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours of undergraduate instruction per academic year. Provide academic and career advising to students. Contribute to the college’s General Education Program, as determined to be appropriate in consultation with the Department and Program Chairs. Provide service to the Department and the College. Engage in ongoing professional development, scholarly, and/or creative activity. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D and/or MFA in Creative Writing (not Literature). ABD will be considered; Experience teaching creative writing at the college level, or equivalent; Demonstrated experience with advancing a learning environment that is conducive to all students. For instance, experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities, course content, and subject matter experts, and working with diverse student populations; Evidence of high-quality creative publications, with a primary publication record in poetry; Experience with student-run literary magazines. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of demonstrated teaching excellence at the undergraduate level; At least one full-length poetry book with a nationally recognized publisher; Ph.D and/or MFA with a focus in poetry. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: a curriculum vitae A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or creative scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by November 15, 2026 . Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/f1d6aae63c7e-assistant-professor-of-environmental-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Environmental Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Environmental Science at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Environmental Science Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Environmental Science %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a full-time, continuing Assistant Professor of Environmental Science position in the Environmental Studies and Data Science Department. This position begins August 1, 2027, and is part of the College's extended contract system, carrying a 24-credit annual teaching load. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. Through leadership in our thriving environmental program, the successful candidate will educate changemakers and contribute to scientific understanding and environmental stewardship in the western North Carolina region. Position Description Ideal candidates will be able to teach introductory environmental science and specialty environmental science courses in the successful candidate’s area of expertise. The faculty member in this position is also expected to advise students in the Environmental Science major, mentor students in undergraduate research projects, and work in close collaboration with the WWC Center for Working Lands team. This is a full-time, continuing faculty position on a 10-month contract in an extended contract system. The Department of Environmental Studies and Data Science is an interdisciplinary learning community, preparing leaders who are able to critically assess, develop, and promote resilient and sustainable futures for life on Earth. The department offers three majors: Sustainable Agriculture and Food Studies (BS or BA), Environmental Science (BS), and Environmental Studies (BA). Program graduates have a strong foundation in the natural sciences and a rich understanding of the social, political and ethical implications of environmental problems and solutions. Approximately 900 acres of ecologically-managed working farm and forestland (including stream and river ecosystems), as well as thousands of acres of surrounding public lands serve as a hands-on educational setting for our program. Successful candidates will have evidence of excellence in and commitment to undergraduate teaching, a track record of relevant scholarly activity, experience in interdisciplinary approaches to environmental studies, and ability to collaboratively contribute to the College’s environmental programs. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours (6 courses) per academic year; typically, 4 credits are assigned to mentor undergraduate research projects (1 course). Provide academic and career advising to Environmental Science students. Mentor student research projects within the Natural Science Undergraduate Research Sequence. Integrate the College’s working lands into teaching and research through collaboration with the Farm, Forest, and Landscape managers. Participate in and contribute to the business of the Environmental Studies Department. Contribute to the college’s General Education Program, as appropriate. Provide service to the college, as well as ongoing professional development and scholarly activity. Minimum Qualifications PhD in Environmental Science, Soil Science, Geology, Hydrology, or related field (ABD will be considered); One year of successful teaching experience; Demonstrated experience with advancing a learning environment that is conducive to all students. For instance, experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities, selection of course content, subject matter experts and working with diverse student populations; Demonstrated commitment to professional growth and scholarship. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of teaching excellence at the undergraduate level; Evidence of experiential and applied learning pedagogy; Experience mentoring undergraduate research; Preferred expertise in hydrology, soil science, or biogeochemistry; Teaching and/or professional experience in traditional ecological knowledge. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: a curriculum vitae A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or creative scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by November 8, 2026. Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/fa17bbc2b6ba-assistant-professor-of-health-sciences": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Health Sciences at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Health Sciences Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Health Sciences %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a faculty position in Health Sciences. This is an Assistant Professor position in an extended contract system with a 24 credit teaching load (typically a 3-3 class load) beginning in August of 2027. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. We seek a broadly trained biologist with specific expertise in human biology and the health sciences, possessing a deep commitment to undergraduate teaching within a liberal arts framework. Preferred qualifications include experience delivering health science coursework and a demonstrated track record of mentoring students through pre-health advising, experiential internships, and undergraduate research. The successful candidate will join the Department of Biology and Chemistry, an interdisciplinary and collaborative learning community that supports majors in Animal Science, Biochemistry, Biology, and Conservation Biology. Courses taught by the candidate will also support students pursuing other majors in the environmental and social sciences. Position Description The Biology and Chemistry Department seeks a dedicated educator and scholar to teach foundational and upper-level coursework in the health and biological sciences. The successful candidate will teach key courses such as Introduction to Health Sciences , General Biology, and Human Biology ( non-majors course), alongside specialized offerings in their area of expertise—such as Cell Biology, Epidemiology, Anatomy and Physiology, or Biostatistics —that support our Biology, Animal Science, and Biochemistry majors. A key responsibility of this role includes developing a signature community-engaged health science course that bridges academic study with local health initiatives. A core component will also be pre-health advising, delivering targeted academic guidance, career mentorship, and specialized programming to pre-Health students. Building on these student mentorship efforts, the candidate will establish an active research program that involves undergraduates, mentoring student capstone projects within the College’s Natural Science Undergraduate Research Sequence. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours of undergraduate instruction per academic year. Provide academic and career advising to students. Contribute to the College’s General Education Program, as determined to be appropriate in consultation with the Department Chair. Provide service to the Department and the College. Engage in ongoing professional development, scholarly and/or creative activity. Develop and teach a core community-engaged health sciences course that connects academic study with community health initiatives and partners. Serve as pre-Health advisor delivering academic advising, career guidance, and targeted programming. Mentor student capstone research projects within the Natural Science Undergraduate Research Sequence. Participate in and contribute to the business of the Biology and Chemistry Department. Develop a research program that involves undergraduate students. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D in Biology, Health Sciences or related field. ABD will be considered. Experience teaching Health Sciences and Biology at the college level, or equivalent. Demonstrated experience with advancing a learning environment that is conducive to all students. For instance, experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities, course content, and subject matter experts, and working with diverse student populations. Experience in the areas of human health science in teaching or research settings. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of demonstrated teaching excellence at the undergraduate level. Demonstrated track record of scholarship in the health sciences, including peer-reviewed publications. Experience developing or teaching community-engaged courses. Experience mentoring and advising undergraduate students pursuing health-related careers. Experience supporting student internships and/or developing external internship partnerships. Experience mentoring undergraduates in research. Evidence of experiential and applied learning pedagogy. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: a curriculum vitae A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or creative scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by Sunday, November 15, 2026 . Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/e9a5e98edd8b-assistant-professor-of-politics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Politics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Politics at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Politics Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Politics %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a faculty position in Politics, in the Department of Public Humanities. This is an Assistant Professor position in an extended contract system with a 24 credit teaching load (typically a 3-3 class load) beginning in August of 2027. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. We seek applicants who are broadly-trained in Politics, Political Science and/or Political Theory; can envision contributing to a new interdisciplinary major integrating History, Philosophy, and Politics; and are committed to teaching and mentoring undergraduates in a liberal arts environment that prioritizes experiential learning. Position Description The Department of Public Humanities seeks a teacher-scholar trained in Politics, Political Science and/or Political Theory broadly-conceived, with an approach that aligns with Warren Wilson’s commitment to experiential teaching and learning intended to prepare graduates for meaningful work, and for effective participation in community and civic life. Applicants from a variety of approaches, such as comparative politics or government, political economy, social/political movements, political communication, law or public policy, etc., are welcome. Specialization in more qualitative, conceptual, textual, practical or historical approaches to the field are preferred over primarily quantitative approaches. The Department of Public Humanities is a collegial, interdisciplinary department committed to the ongoing vital importance of the humanities in the 21st century. The successful applicant will offer Introduction to American Government, and other introductory and advanced courses within their expertise in support of the new Ethics, Politics and Public Life major (a major that combines History, Philosophy and Politics, while permitting concentrations in each), and the College’s General Education program. Successful applicants will also be prepared to contribute courses or programming in support of the College’s gift-funded “Democracy in Action” initiative. Over time the new hire will also mentor Politics-focused capstone projects, which will include public-facing elements. In addition, Warren Wilson faculty have the opportunity to design and offer faculty-led study away courses, first-year seminars, and community-engaged courses. The College offers funding and programming to support faculty growth as teachers, scholars and practitioners. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours of undergraduate instruction per academic year. Provide academic and career advising to students. Contribute to the college’s General Education Program, as determined to be appropriate in consultation with the Department Chair. Provide service to the Department and the College. Engage in ongoing professional development, scholarly and/or creative activity. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D in Politics, Political Theory, Political Science or related field. ABD will be considered. Experience teaching. Demonstrated experience with advancing a learning environment that is conducive to all learners. For instance, experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities, course content, and subject matter experts, and working with diverse student populations. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of demonstrated teaching excellence at the undergraduate level. Evidence of a pedagogical approach that prioritizes experiential and applied learning. Experience of successful contribution to multi/interdisciplinary studies in the humanities. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: a curriculum vitae A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or creative scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by Sunday, November 8, 2026 . Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/558d5b2923e5-assistant-professor-of-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Psychology Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Psychology %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a faculty position in Developmental Psychology. This is an Assistant Professor position in an extended contract system with a 24 credit teaching load (typically a 3-3 class load) beginning in August of 2027. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. We seek candidates with a background in developmental psychology who are strongly committed to teaching, mentoring, and student-engaged research in an undergraduate liberal arts environment that prioritizes experiential learning. Position Description Applicants must be able to offer courses in Introduction to Psychology, Developmental Psychology (for example: Infant & Child Development, Adolescence & Emerging Adulthood), Research Methods, and upper level courses in their speciality. As part of their regular teaching load, all faculty also supervise senior capstone projects involving research and practicum placements. The successful candidate will also have the opportunity to collaborate on study-away courses, first-year seminars, and community-based projects. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours (i.e., 3-3 course load) of undergraduate instruction per academic year. Provide academic and career advising to students. Contribute to the college’s General Education Program, as determined to be appropriate in consultation with the Department Chair. Provide service to the Department and the College. Engage in ongoing professional development and scholarly activity. Maintain and develop partnerships (e.g., schools, non-profits) to support community engaged learning in the developmental course sequence, internships, and capstone research. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Psychology or related field. ABD will be considered; Experience teaching psychology at the college level, or equivalent; Demonstrated experience with advancing a learning environment that is conducive to all students. For instance, experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities, course content, and subject matter experts, and working with diverse student populations. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of demonstrated teaching excellence at the undergraduate level; Evidence of a pedagogical approach that prioritizes experiential and applied learning; Ability to contribute to and support a collegewide initiative toward an interdisciplinary Health Science major. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: a curriculum vitae A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by November 1, 2026. Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/ac3b8099b275-director-of-financial-aid": {
      "title": "Director of Financial Aid",
      "description": "Director of Financial Aid at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Director of Financial Aid Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Financial Aid %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Director of Financial Aid %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College is seeking an experienced and strategic leader to serve as the Director of Student Financial Aid. The Director will lead the College’s student financial aid functions to support the institution’s broader enrollment and retention goals through a consistent, student-focused approach that balances regulatory compliance with practical problem solving and efficiency. The Director reports to and works alongside the Vice President of Enrollment Management on strategic innovations and improvements within and across the division, and supervises a staff consisting of a full-time Associate Director and a shared full-time Student Financial Services Advisor, with a split schedule between Student Accounts and Financial Aid. The Director of Financial Aid also coordinates veterans’ benefits as the School Certifying Officer. The individual in this role is expected to be a leader with a visible presence on campus, with a willingness to engage in a highly collaborative (and fun!) work environment as the resident financial aid expert. The ideal candidate brings both technical expertise, knowledge of best practices, and guidance to  ensure students receive accurate, timely support while the College maintains strong compliance to navigate regulatory changes within the industry. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested individuals should read further to learn more about the College and the responsibilities for this full-time, exempt position. Applicants are required to submit a resume and cover letter that describes how your previous experience will contribute to the role and the mission of Warren Wilson College. Applications will be accepted and reviewed on a rolling basis as they are received. Priority consideration will be given to candidates who submit their materials by Sunday, November 1, 2026. All applications must be received electronically. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Three to five years of previous Financial Aid experience, with progressive responsibility and leadership. Demonstrated fluency with CRM systems and data analytics. Experience with financial aid reporting to include IPEDS, ACTS, FVT, and FISAP. Working knowledge of tuition discounting practices, packaging philosophies, and Title programs. Previous experience managing and leading teams of people; focus on team culture with a strong sense of urgency and accountability. Demonstrated experience working effectively with a wide range of constituents from diverse backgrounds and perspectives. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in a related field. Experience PowerFAIDS, Jenzabar and Slate or similar systems. Experience in a non-profit higher education institution. Successful Candidates will demonstrate knowledge of: Federal, state, institutional, and local sources of student financial assistance. Institutional/organizational, policies, procedures, and administrative practices generally administered in higher education. Finance, accounting, and management. Principles of training and providing work direction; developing and mentoring early- to mid-career team members. Best practices in financial aid needs analysis, packaging and awarding. The following financial aid systems/programs: ED Connect, COD, CFNC, ELM, NSLDS, FSA Partner Connect. Rules, regulations, requirements, and policies related to enrollment, financial aid, and student accounts. Interpersonal skills using tact, patience, and courtesy. Key Responsibilities Directs all financial aid functions with the purpose of enrolling and maintaining a diverse student body and institutional eligibility for continued participation in Federal and State aid programs. Creates and maintains a customer service based focus for the Office of Financial Aid and coordinates efforts with the College Business Office, Registrar, Information Technology, Admissions, MFA/Craft and International Programs as well as other offices on campus. Directs the administration of financial aid; ensures applicants receive assistance in the application process for loans, grants and scholarships. Makes awards to current and incoming students while adhering to Title IV regulations, state and federal guidelines, private non-profit agencies, institutional packaging policy and budget parameters. Oversees the administration of private, state, and/or federal loan programs; coordinates the awarding of need-based scholarships/grants and the renewal of merit-based awards. Directs the completion of federal work college reports and determines eligibility for students. Directs the completion of reports to request funding, document expenditures or marketing. Reports include but are not limited to FISAP, IPEDS, CFNC for NC funds, Pell, and direct lending. Directs the personnel activities to include hiring, development, training, merit recommendations, promotions, transfers, and performance evaluations. Directs and oversees technology systems that financial aid uses in the awarding process, this includes PowerFAIDS and Jenzabar EX. Plans and directs the presentation and enforcement of policies and forms (i.e. Withdrawal and Refund, Satisfactory Academic Progress Standards, Verification forms, etc.). Participates in off-campus training when available and as needed. Attends the Department of Education’s annual FAS Conference (fall), any NC state meeting (usually mandatory). Attends training and professional development meetings as needed to stay abreast of regulations and changes. Represents Warren Wilson College during audits: Federal Program Review, North Carolina State Approving Agency, North Carolina Higher Education Assistance Authority, Department of Veteran Affairs, and the annual external audit prepared for the United States Department of Education and the State of North Carolina. Review appeal cases, special circumstances, and college-wide financial aid policies. Serves on institutional committees as needed. Develops, maintains, and works within a realistic budget, with specific attention to contract work for special projects and ongoing department needs. Reconciles credit card statements, collects and submits all departmental check requests to the office of Administration and Finance. Performs other duties as assigned. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an academically and culturally diverse faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and focus on inclusive excellence, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. We welcome applications from individuals who have experience working with diverse student populations. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer . %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/becbd1db793c-public-safety-officer": {
      "title": "Public Safety Officer",
      "description": "Public Safety Officer at Warren Wilson Employees Public Safety Officer Asheville, NC Full-Time Public Safety Job Openings Public Safety Officer Apply To Position Apply Using LinkedIn Warren Wilson College is seeking a service-oriented Public Safety Officer to join our team. We’re looking for a dedicated team player who can collaborate effectively with a variety of campus constituents and work well with diverse populations in a college setting. The ideal candidate will have prior experience in public safety, law enforcement, or the military. The primary responsibility of this role is to help maintain a safe and secure environment for faculty, staff, students, and guests on the Warren Wilson College campus. Strong customer service skills are essential, as our officers are often the first point of contact for community members who need support, guidance, or reassurance. This position requires flexibility, including availability for overnight shifts. We are currently hiring for our night shift schedule of 7:00 PM to 7:00 AM. Training Schedule: Initial training schedules may vary based on staffing needs, trainer availability, and departmental requirements. Candidates should be prepared to complete training on a schedule that may differ from their regularly assigned shift. We accept applications on a rolling basis, and qualified candidates may be contacted as openings become available. This posting will remain active to maintain a pool of applicants for future opportunities. Compensation: This is an hourly position with a pay rate of $21.50 per hour. Shifts worked between 7:00 PM and 7:00 AM are eligible for an additional $1.50 per hour shift differential. Interested individuals should read further to learn more about the qualifications and job responsibilities for this full-time, hourly position. Applicants are required to submit a resume that highlights their prior experience working with individuals from a diverse range of identities and experiences. All applications must be submitted electronically. Please note that relocation assistance is not available for this position. Minimum Qualifications High school diploma or general education degree (GED.) Evidence of ethical behaviors and adhering to established procedures. Demonstrated ability to be cooperative, collaborative, and inclusive team player. Demonstrated experience working effectively with people from all backgrounds and perspectives. Valid North Carolina Driver's license in good standing. Must have the ability to obtain campus licensing once hired. Certifications in CPR and First Aid. (Can be obtained after hiring). Preferred Qualifications Associates degree from an accredited college/university. ​ Prior work history of public safety, security, military, or law enforcement. Key Responsibilities Inspects buildings and grounds to observe and secure facilities against fire and other situations of potential loss to Warren Wilson College. Admits and releases authorized persons, removes unauthorized persons and provides access control for approved personnel. Responds, investigates and documents disturbances and/or incidents, takes necessary and appropriate actions to maintain order, safety and to protect property.  Cooperates with campus staff and external law enforcement agencies while conducting investigations. Testifies in court, if applicable or required. Provides escort services as requested for students and visitors to Warren Wilson College facilities and parking lots. Participates in campus wide events that enhance the Public Safety Department’s interaction and visibility amongst the campus community. Abides, follows and enforces Warren Wilson College policies, applicable local, state and federal laws and regulations. Enforces campus parking regulations by issuing tickets and controlling traffic as needed. Performs special assignments including staffing of external/internal events and protecting Warren Wilson College equipment and property. Is required to operate Warren Wilson College vehicles including, but not limited to vans, golf carts and any other motorized vehicles safely. Must possess the ability to create comprehensive and concise written reports and other types of institutional documentation as necessary while applying principles & correct usage of grammar, punctuation and spelling. Must possess the ability to use a laptop/desktop and a variety of computer software to enter, modify, retrieve and track incident, hazard, statistical and other data, and to compose and prepare correspondence, surveys, memoranda, routine reports and other written material. Commitment to diversity and inclusion. Other duties as assigned. Working Conditions Must be physically capable of entering all campus facilities, able to drive appropriate vehicles, and work indoor and outdoor in all weather conditions, including walking and traversing trails and College property. ​Must be capable of lifting up to 50 pounds. ​Must be capable of standing or walking for prolonged periods, walking up and down inclines, climbing stairs, and occasionally running, stooping, lunging, reaching, crawling, or other physical activities required to respond to calls. Our mission: Warren Wilson College’s distinctive approach to education intentionally integrates academics, work, and community engagement to cultivate curiosity, empathy, and integrity. We empower graduates to pursue meaningful careers and lead purposeful lives dedicated to a just, equitable, and sustainable world. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an academically and culturally diverse faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and focus on inclusive excellence, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. We welcome applications from individuals who have experience working with diverse student populations. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. Apply To Position Apply Using LinkedIn Powered by breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/7ca82216f319-assistant-registrar": {
      "title": "Assistant Registrar",
      "description": "Assistant Registrar at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Registrar Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Registrar %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Registrar %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College invites applications for an Assistant Registrar. Under the leadership of the College Registrar, this position supports the Registrar's Office and the students, faculty, administrators, and staff who rely on it. The Assistant Registrar manages day-to-day office communications, student registration and degree data, and the cross-departmental processes for student withdrawals, exits, and readmissions. The position is also the primary point of contact for the College's School of Record partners and helps the Registrar grow those partnerships and improve their processes. Warren Wilson is a federally designated Work College, and the Registrar's Office includes two student workers. The Assistant Registrar assists the office's crew supervisor with the student work crew. Our office is a collaborative, student-centered environment where team members work closely together to solve problems, support one another, and continuously improve processes that help students succeed. The ideal candidate is detail-oriented, comfortable with student information systems and data, and committed to providing excellent customer service. This person solves problems practically, keeps student information confidential, and works well with a wide range of people. This is a full-time, 12-month, non-exempt position, working 40 hours per week, Monday through Friday.  The role offers the possibility of working in a hybrid capacity, incorporating both remote and on-site responsibilities to support flexibility and collaboration. Interested individuals should read further to learn more about the College and the responsibilities of this position. Applicants are required to submit a resume and cover letter that describes how their previous experience will contribute to the mission and Progressive Promise of Warren Wilson College. First consideration will be given to candidates who apply by November 8, 2026 . All applications must be received electronically. Please be aware no relocation assistance is available for this position. Minimum Qualifications Associate's degree in office administration, business administration, or a related field. Three years of administrative support experience in a high-volume office. One year of experience using enterprise resource planning (ERP) software or a student information system (SIS). Demonstrated excellence in verbal and written communication, and attention to detail and accuracy. Proven customer service, organizational, and interpersonal skills, and the ability to maintain confidentiality. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with people from a diverse range of identities and experiences. High proficiency with Google Workspace and Microsoft Office, and with database management and report creation. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. Prior experience working in a registrar's office. Strong analytical skills and the ability to solve problems logically and creatively. Knowledge of rules, regulations, and laws regarding student records. Previous supervisory experience with traditional college-age students. Previous experience with the Jenzabar Student Information System. Key Responsibilities Communications and records Manage incoming email, phone, and walk-in inquiries, responding or assigning staff as appropriate. Manage outgoing student communications from the Registrar's Office. Maintain the student and employee important dates calendars. Manage student degree and registration data, including waitlists, add/drop forms, course withdrawals, and major and minor declaration changes. Systems and data processing Manage the workflow for student withdrawals, exits, and readmissions across departments. Manage FERPA permissions in Jenzabar. Manage communications and Jenzabar processes for the MFA and MACS programs. Process placement exam results, and manage the ACT and waitlist processes after registration and add/drop. School of Record Serve as primary point of contact for School of Record partners. Manage School of Record and Consortium Agreement workflows, including communication with campus partners and enrolled students, student record processing, and transcripting. Support the College Registrar in growing School of Record partnerships and building better processes for them. Team and professional development Assist the Registrar's crew supervisor with the student work crew. Participate in professional development and training. Perform other duties as assigned. About Warren Wilson College Our Mission: Warren Wilson College's distinctive approach to education intentionally integrates academics, work, and community engagement to cultivate curiosity, empathy, and integrity. We empower graduates to pursue meaningful careers and lead purposeful lives dedicated to a just, equitable, and sustainable world. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that all students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an engaged faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and the Progressive Promise for all, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    },
    "https://warren-wilson.breezy.hr/p/83254a9f5b56-assistant-professor-of-literature-media-and-communications": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Literature, Media and Communications",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Literature, Media and Communications at Warren Wilson %HEADER_EMPLOYEES% Assistant Professor of Literature, Media and Communications Asheville, NC %LABEL_POSITION_TYPE_FULL_TIME% Academic Affairs %BREADCRUMB_JOB_OPENINGS% Assistant Professor of Literature, Media and Communications %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% Warren Wilson College welcomes applicants for a faculty position in Literature and/or Media and Communications. This is an Assistant Professor position in an extended contract system with a 24 credit teaching load (typically a 3-3 class load) beginning in August of 2027. On Warren Wilson's extended contract system, after an initial 3 year period of annual reviews, faculty receive extended-term contracts that provide for continuing appointment, and are linked to academic rank review and promotion. Position Description The Public Humanities Department is an interdisciplinary learning community, preparing students with transferable skills more critical than ever. Within the Department, this position will serve two closely aligned majors: 1) Media and Communications and 2) Literature. It will also provide leadership for the College’s writing requirement in general education and the Writing Center “Crew,” with appropriate workload adjustment. These majors focus on strong written and oral communication, argument, close reading, problem solving, big questions, diverse voices, critical thinking, and public facing work. Faculty in the two majors work closely together, allowing students to count credits toward the major from both disciplines. Both majors also include courses in other departments and collaborate deeply with other humanities subjects, in particular. There are opportunities for internships and community engagement in both majors. Faculty in the department extend the classroom and campus through close connections with community partners. Responsibilities Teach 24 credit hours of undergraduate instruction per academic year. Provide academic and career advising to students. Contribute to the college’s General Education Program, as determined to be appropriate in consultation with the Department Chair. Provide service to the Department and the College. Engage in ongoing professional development, scholarly and/or creative activity. Teach six courses in the successful disciplinary home, ideally three in Literature and three in Media and Communications. Provide leadership for the college’s writing requirement in general education and the Writing Center “Crew”. Provide academic and career advising to Literature and to Media and Communications majors. Mentor students as they prepare senior capstone projects. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Literature or Communications. ABD will be considered; Ability to contribute to both the COM and the LIT majors; Evidence of experience in teaching undergraduates; Demonstrated experience with advancing diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging in learning spaces or experience incorporating inclusive teaching modalities and working with diverse student populations. Preferred Qualifications One or more years of full-time teaching experience as the instructor of record at the undergraduate level; Evidence of experiential and applied learning pedagogy; A scholarly and/or teaching background in multiple literary periods or genres; Evidence of scholarly publication and productivity; Evidence of production in media and/or communications. About the College: Warren Wilson College is located on a beautiful 1,135-acre campus in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Asheville, North Carolina. We enroll approximately 750 undergraduates and 100 graduates. Rooted in liberal arts education, the College provides educational programs that foster students’ curiosity, empathy, and integrity to prepare graduates to engage in groundbreaking scholarship and creative work; to pursue meaningful careers with professionalism; and to lead purposeful lives dedicated to fostering a just, equitable, and sustainable world. As the National Leader for Learning in Action, our Progressive Promise articulates our innovative educational approach that intentionally integrates academics, campus work, and community engagement for a rich, challenging student experience. Our students matriculate through deliberately scaffolded courses and signature capstone experiences that combine intellectual foundations with experiential skill development and problem solving. Interested candidates should provide the following documents to complete their application: A curriculum vitae. A cover letter that describes how the applicant’s teaching, research, and/or creative scholarly experience prepares the applicant to contribute to Warren Wilson’s experiential learning and a diverse student body in a residential work college setting Additional materials may be requested of applicants whose candidacy advances. Priority consideration will be given to candidates who apply by November 29, 2026 . Electronic submission is required. We are committed to a culture of educational access and to ensuring that students and employees from a diverse range of identities and experiences will thrive in this community. We seek and support an academically and culturally diverse faculty and staff with a genuine interest in our innovative approach to education and focus on inclusive excellence, as well as a willingness to fully participate in the life of the College. We welcome applications from individuals who have experience working with diverse student populations. Warren Wilson College is proud to be an equal opportunity employer. %BUTTON_APPLY_TO_POSITION% %BUTTON_APPLY_USING_LINKED_IN% %FOOTER_POWERED_BY% breezy"
    }
  },
